#
# Kernel log on the monitor instead of a desktop.
#
# The compositor owns the display, and when it is working that is what you
# want. When it is not -- a machine that boots to a black screen and has no
# serial port, which is most desktops -- there is nothing to look at and
# nothing to go on.
#
# This turns the compositor off entirely so the framebuffer console takes the
# screen the ordinary way and prints the boot log. It is the image to write
# when the normal one shows nothing:
#
#       sh build.sh --usb arch/x64 debug_none limbo desktop console
#
# The whole target list, not the profile alone: naming any profile replaces
# the tail of the default set rather than adding to it, and a build without
# arch/x64 stops at the assembly step.
#
# Nothing else changes, so anything that goes wrong before graphics comes up
# goes wrong identically and is visible this time.
#
# The other way round -- keeping the compositor and putting fbdev back under
# it with drm_client_lib.active=fbdev -- would say why Canvas failed rather
# than only that it did. It hangs the boot with nothing on either console,
# and why is not known; the two clients appear to fight over the display.
#
CONFIG_MOONWATER_CANVAS=n

# `server` is the same hard Canvas boundary under the name intended for normal
# headless/console deployments. This profile remains the documented graphical
# recovery spelling.

# With no compositor to keep it away, the fbdev client is wanted after all.
CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION=y
CONFIG_FB=y
CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_VT=y
CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE=y

# The built-in command line otherwise tells the fbdev client to stay away,
# which would leave this image with nothing on screen either.
#> cmdline -drm_client_lib.active=
